WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:08.480
Perhaps no single artist has had a greater impact&nbsp;
on how we imagine alien life than Wayne Barlowe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:14.320
Widely considered the father of modern Speculative&nbsp;
Biology, Barlowe has worked for decades as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:21.200
creature designer for everything from Avatar to&nbsp;
Pacific Rim. Yet perhaps Barlow’s most monumental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:27.040
alien world comes from his legendary book&nbsp;
‘Expedition’ — which depicts an extraterrestrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.720
ecosystem with so much originality and detail&nbsp;
that reading it truly feels like getting lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.640
on another planet. The book was even made into a&nbsp;
TV special for the Discovery Channel called ‘Alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.760
Planet,’ which was honestly one of my favorite&nbsp;
things to watch as a kid. The world at the center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.120
of the book and documentary — called ‘Darwin&nbsp;
IV’ — is one I’ve wanted to document myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.760
for a long time. So, for this entry into the&nbsp;
archive, we’ll take a dive-deep into the biology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.920
and ecosystems of Barlowe’s one-of-a-kind planet.&nbsp;
In honor of the book’s logbook-style storytelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:07.680
this entry will be told as a firsthand account&nbsp;
of an expedition across Darwin IV’s surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.680
And if you find the planet as incredible&nbsp;
as I do, I’ve got links below where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.800
can purchase his book and support his&nbsp;
work. Now, let’s begin our expedition…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.080
My story begins with this single, blurry photo&nbsp;
taken by a faster-than-light probe exploring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.600
the far reaches of the galaxy. The image shows&nbsp;
alien life is present in the fourth planet of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.680
the distant Darwinian System. But what kind&nbsp;
of lifeform is this alien? How large is it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.720
And what other lifeforms might it coexist&nbsp;
with? There’s only one way to find out…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:48.000
After a journey of many lightyears, I arrive at&nbsp;
Darwin IV. From orbit, my first scans indicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.160
the planet is a dry, hothouse world. The only&nbsp;
visible body of water is a peculiar, purple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:01.280
ocean of unknown properties. Scans also indicate&nbsp;
Darwin IV possesses an oxygen-rich atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.120
and less than two-thirds Earth’s gravity —&nbsp;
meaning life on Darwin IV may well be gigantic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.960
I’ll have to proceed carefully… Touching down&nbsp;
in the northern hemisphere, I find a sweeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:20.400
grassland dominated by titanic plant structures&nbsp;
called Gourd Trees. Nearly fifteen stories high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.000
these trees stand on a matrix of root-like&nbsp;
stilts. If these are the plants of this world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.440
the animals might be true titans… Not far from the&nbsp;
grove, something is squatting low to the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.800
Consulting the field guide of&nbsp;
an explorer who came before me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.880
I identify this creates as an Arrowtongue. While&nbsp;
it doesn’t appear to have eyes, a series of low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.480
clicks tells me it can ‘see’ with an organic&nbsp;
sonar system — and likely knows I’m here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.960
Rearing up to its full height, the Arrowtongue&nbsp;
reveals it’s a t-rex sized apex predator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.720
Unlike all top earth predators, however, the&nbsp;
Arrowtongue doesn’t possess jaws. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:06.480
it feeds with a straw-like appendage on its mouth&nbsp;
which is common among all ‘liquivores’ — a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.840
of tube-mouthed predators that includes the&nbsp;
Arrowtongue. And this Arrowtongue seems hungry…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.320
…But not for me. The preferred food of an&nbsp;
Arrowtongue are the Gyrosprinters — herbivores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.160
with highly unusual body-plans. While they spend&nbsp;
much of their time resting, they’re the fastest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.160
all land animals here. Yet the Gyrosprinters’&nbsp;
ability to careen across the grassland at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.920
incredible speeds is a tricky thing, considering&nbsp;
that they only have two legs. To keep balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.600
they possess a set of hyper-developed ‘halteres’&nbsp;
— club-shaped organs which earth animals like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.600
craneflies use to keep their bodies stabilized&nbsp;
during motion. A key trait for the Gyrosprinter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.440
which must always be ready to flee. Speaking of&nbsp;
which, it’s best I leave these two to the chase…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.160
Not far from this unfolding drama, I spot a&nbsp;
leathery Rayback trotting across the grassland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:06.080
A smaller relative of the colossal Arrowtongue,&nbsp;
the quick, solitary Raybacks are in many ways the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:12.720
cheetahs of this alien savanna. Yet the Raybacks’&nbsp;
most notable features — their exaggerated dorsal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.800
spires — are something of a biological enigma.&nbsp;
If we look at Earth animals for clues as to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:25.120
function of these spines, we find some chameleons&nbsp;
possess similar bony ridges to appear larger from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:31.280
the side and to intimidate rivals. It’s a curious&nbsp;
strategy, but if it works for the chameleon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:37.040
it’s certainly possible it would work on Darwin&nbsp;
IV for the Rayback. Traveling northwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.720
I’m lucky enough to glimpse a mighty hunter&nbsp;
standing triumphant over its latest quarry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:49.520
This ambush predator is called a Prairie-Ram, and&nbsp;
true to its name, this particular liquivore hunts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.440
by ramming its prey with its pointed skull. While&nbsp;
no earth predators hunt in quite the same way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:01.680
some species have evolved to ram into each&nbsp;
other in territorial displays. In particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.360
big horn sheep have evolved skulls and horns that&nbsp;
can absorb tremendous concussive force thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.200
their internal structure. We can theorize that the&nbsp;
interior of the Prairie-Rams skull likely has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:20.080
similar makeup… At the edge of the grasslands,&nbsp;
I watch a duo of mid-sized herbivores — called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:26.240
prismalopes — graze in the shade of a curious&nbsp;
tree. But it’s not the prismalopes I’m focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:32.560
on. Upon closer inspection, the apparent ‘tree’&nbsp;
behind them doesn’t seem to be a plant at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:39.520
This is a butcher tree, a lifeform with a deadly&nbsp;
secret. It’s scent lures in herbivores, and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:46.000
one gets close, the butchertree spears it with&nbsp;
its branch-daggers. Scans conform this lifeform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:53.440
is closer to animal than plant. I better keep my&nbsp;
distance… Night is now falling on Darwin IV. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.920
in the darkness, the Prongheads stalk the savanna.&nbsp;
These well-muscled pack hunters are the wolves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:06.960
of Darwin IV, and move with surprising speed.&nbsp;
Cresting a hill, I see what they’re hunting. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:13.120
gyrosprinter from earlier survived the chase with&nbsp;
the Arrowtongue, but is now in even more danger!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.720
Racing across the plains, the gyrosprinter is&nbsp;
incredibly fast… but the Prongheads have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.800
numbers advantage. Unlike the Arrowtongue, they&nbsp;
can coordinate in a pack to cut off their prey…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.560
But this gyrosprinter is a survivor, and&nbsp;
manages to give them the slip. On Darwin IV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:37.440
it pays to be the quickest. With the morning now&nbsp;
just a few hours away, I’ve made a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:43.680
discovery. I’ve followed a lone Rayback beyond&nbsp;
the savanna to the edge of a mysterious forest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.440
This new environment might be home to all manner&nbsp;
of distinct lifeforms. But the only way to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:55.840
for sure is to venture deeper… I don’t have&nbsp;
to go far. On the periphery of the forest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:03.040
a huge creature shakes the ground, so tall that&nbsp;
it towers over the trees. This is a Groveback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:09.200
one of the largest species native to Darwin IV.&nbsp;
Strange tree-like structures grow from its back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:14.480
which might help the Groveback remain camouflaged&nbsp;
while it slumbers. Given the Groveback’s size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.640
however, it seems unlikely this creature would&nbsp;
fear predators. Instead, as the sun rises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.760
I wonder if perhaps the Groveback has a symbiotic&nbsp;
relationship with the plants on its back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.880
gaining energy from their photosynthesis much&nbsp;
like an earth ‘Leaf Sheep’ — a rare type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:36.000
sea slug that almost looks like a miniature&nbsp;
Groveback from certain angles. In any case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.040
the Grovebacks are truly awe-inspiring. I’ll have&nbsp;
to make sure they don’t step on me, though...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.640
Deeper into the forest, I spot a curious lifeform&nbsp;
reclining on a branch far above me. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.800
Daggerwrist, an agile arboreal predator that&nbsp;
rules the upper treetops thanks to its deadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:01.680
bladed proboscis — which is the closest thing to a&nbsp;
functioning jaw on Darwin IV. In a burst of speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.040
the Daggerwrist leaps to another tree, scaling&nbsp;
the side of the trunk by using the claw-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:12.720
arms that are its namesake almost like climbing&nbsp;
spikes. And in the canopy, the Daggerwrist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:18.880
rejoins a troop of its own kind. This family&nbsp;
unit is comprised of a young infant and two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:24.960
parents — and their pack behavior showcases a&nbsp;
new side of Darwin life: social interaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:31.280
While Daggerwrists might be deadly predators, like&nbsp;
earth monkeys it seems they are sociable, living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.880
in troops of one or more families and looking out&nbsp;
for each other. After reuniting with its troop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.000
the Daggerwrist goes on the hunt once more,&nbsp;
and I get to witness it leaping from a tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.600
and gliding to the ground using sails under its&nbsp;
arms — soaring in a manner quite similar to many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:54.240
species of gliding earth animals. The hunt doesn’t&nbsp;
last long. The Daggerwrist is a brutal hunter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:09:00.800
and an animal I should be wary of. On the other&nbsp;
edge of the forest, I see a lone Groveback that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:06.240
seems to be under attack. The air is filled&nbsp;
with a swarm of needle like predators, which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.840
launching themselves at the titantic Groveback&nbsp;
from all directions. These are Beachquills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:16.560
and while it seems impossible for these small&nbsp;
lifeforms to take down such a behemoth, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:23.840
Groveback is clearly affected by their onslaught.&nbsp;
I realize the Beachquills must be highly toxic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:28.800
It might seem strange that tiny organisms&nbsp;
like Beachquills could be so venomous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.400
but animals like the tiny Bluespotted Ribbontail&nbsp;
Ray are some of the most venomous lifeforms in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:41.600
oceans — despite their small size. So, it makes&nbsp;
sense that a colony of Beachquills working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:47.840
tandem would be able to take down all number&nbsp;
of organisms that wander into their domain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:54.800
For the Beachquills mark the edge of a new biome —&nbsp;
one home to far greater dangers than the forest...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:01.200
Two days into my expedition, I’ve reached a&nbsp;
sea of shifting purple liquid, the properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:08.080
of which are highly mysterious. This landscape&nbsp;
appears to be the same ocean I saw from space…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:14.240
which now doesn’t seem to be an ocean at all. With&nbsp;
caution, I take a sample of this strange mass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:20.640
Looking under a field microscope, I find this&nbsp;
ocean isn’t made of water molecules — but living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:28.240
cells. This entire ‘ocean’ is a single, massive&nbsp;
lifeform — an ‘Amoebic Sea’ that blankets about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:34.960
5% of the surface of planet Darwin IV. Looking&nbsp;
towards the horizon, I can see this vast colony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:41.840
stretches far into the distance, and is a biome&nbsp;
in and of itself. A question grows in my mind:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:48.880
what lifeforms have evolved to live upon this&nbsp;
sentient ocean? A low roar thunders across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:55.440
sea. The mightiest creatures I will ever see&nbsp;
are approaching. At last, they come into view.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:02.720
These are Emperor Sea Striders, the largest&nbsp;
lifeforms I’ve discovered on my voyage — towering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.600
over the Amoebic Sea like living mountains.&nbsp;
Without a doubt, these are the creatures the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:13.120
probe first glimpsed that started the entire&nbsp;
expedition — but seeing them in person is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:18.880
entirely different experience. In order&nbsp;
to sustain such an awe-inspiring size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.240
the Sea Strider’s biology is one of a kind&nbsp;
— particularly their feeding system. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.840
Sea Striders possess two mouth-like openings&nbsp;
located on their feet, allowing them to feed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:35.200
on the limitless Amoebic Sea while they stride&nbsp;
across it. It’s the perfect food source for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:41.760
such a vast lifeform. And though a storm rages&nbsp;
around them, the Emperor Sea Striders march on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:49.280
undaunted. In just a short time, I’ve seen more&nbsp;
wonders than I can count on the surface of Darwin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:55.360
IV. Yet I suspect the mysteries of this planet are&nbsp;
just beginning… I’ll venture farther across Darwin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.360 --> 00:12:01.680
IV in Part Two of this expedition, investigating&nbsp;
the more extreme and often more dangerous life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:06.960
of the skies and polar regions. If you have&nbsp;
a particular creature you want me to be sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.720
to cover, put it in the comments. If you find&nbsp;
the life of Darwin IV as fascinating as I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:17.680
I’d encourage you to purchase the book and check&nbsp;
out more of Wayne Barlowe’s incredible work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:23.280
Once again, links are in the description. And&nbsp;
as always, thanks for watching. If you enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:29.440
this entry, please lend your support to help make&nbsp;
these videos possible by liking, subscribing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:37.840
hitting the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

